WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.580
One of the most-used pieces of notation in biochemistry began as a typographic fix.

2
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:16.180
Measuring hydrogen ions threw off columns of decimals, written out and recompared

3
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.760
until the bookkeeping itself became the bottleneck, so a brewery chemist compressed

4
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.400
the mess into a logarithm.

5
00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:26.000
He did not think he had discovered anything.

6
00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:33.430
Across more than 160 pages laid out electrode by electrode, indicator

7
00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:39.310
by indicator, the choice that would outlast him appears exactly once, without

8
00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:45.230
emphasis: "For the number representing the exponent of this power, I have chosen the

9
00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:49.150
symbol pH." Two letters, buried.

10
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:56.240
What his measurements did show, and what he did underline, was that enzymes do not

11
00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:02.800
care how much acid is present in total only about the concentration of free protons.

12
00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:09.753
He proved it on the same invertase reaction that had defeated Victor Henri, whose

13
00:01:09.753 --> 00:01:15.513
earlier results were leaky because no pH scale yet existed to fix the acidity.

14
00:01:16.613 --> 00:01:22.413
Plotted on Sørensen's new logarithmic axis, the data showed a sharp activity peak

15
00:01:22.413 --> 00:01:23.773
the old units had hidden.
